Yes. Identity acts like a prior, an attention filter, and a control policy.

What you identify with influences:

* what you notice;
* which events feel threatening or inviting;
* what explanations seem plausible;
* which actions feel “like me”;
* what evidence you accept or ignore;
* which repetitions eventually become character.

\text{identity}
\rightarrow \text{attention}
\rightarrow \text{interpretation}
\rightarrow \text{action}
\rightarrow \text{consequence}
\rightarrow \text{reinforced identity}

That loop can construct a lived reality without magically rewriting external reality. Identify as powerless and opportunities may become invisible; identify as invincible and genuine danger may become invisible. Both identities distort through selective perception.

Drago’s counterpunch: reality is not infinitely negotiable. Gravity, other minds, consequences, and chance retain veto power. Identity shapes your interface with the world—not the entire world itself.

So hold identity as a steering instrument, not a prison or proof:

Choose what you serve, practice the person capable of serving it, and let reality correct the costume.